Let me reconsider. The crash is in `bfind` reading an uninitialized value. Let me think about what set could have uninitialized `page_map` entries.

Actually, let me reconsider the `hb_set_t` struct. It has `hb_vector_t<page_map_t, 1> page_map;` and `hb_vector_t<page_t, 1> pages;`. The `hb_vector_t` has `Type static_array[StaticSize]`. When `init()` is called, `arrayZ = static_array`, `allocated = 1`, `len = 0`.

Now, `hb_set_create` uses `hb_object_create<hb_set_t>()` which uses `calloc`. So the entire `hb_set_t` struct is zero-initialized. So `static_array[0]` is zeroed. Good.

But wait, there might be sets that are NOT created via `hb_object_create`. Let me look at how sets are used in the subset code. For example, `hb_subset_plan_t` has `plan->unicodes = hb_set_create()`. And `initial_gids_to_retain = hb_set_create()`. These are all created via `hb_set_create`.

Hmm. Let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is in the `hb_set_t::next` function or `page_for` being called on a set that has `page_map.len > 0` but the entries are uninitialized.

Actually, let me reconsider the `page_for_insert` function more carefully. There's a subtle bug:

```c
inline page_t *page_for_insert (hb_codepoint_t g)
{
    page_map_t map = {get_major (g), pages.len};
    unsigned int i;
    if (!page_map.bfind (map, &i))
    {
      if (!resize (pages.len + 1))
        return nullptr;

      pages[map.index].init0 ();
      memmove (&page_map[i + 1], &page_map[i], (page_map.len - 1 - i) * sizeof (page_map[0]));
      page_map[i] = map;
    }
    return &pages[page_map[i].index];
}
```

Wait, `resize(pages.len + 1)` calls `hb_set_t::resize` which resizes BOTH `pages` and `page_map` to `pages.len + 1`. But `map.index = pages.len` (the OLD value). After resize, `pages.len` is `pages.len + 1`. So `map.index` points to the last page. `pages[map.index].init0()` initializes it. Good.

But here's the issue: `page_map.len` is now `pages.len + 1` (after resize). The `memmove` shifts entries. But wait, `page_map.len` was just resized to `pages.len + 1`. So `page_map.len - 1 - i` is the number of entries to shift. This should be correct.

Hmm, let me reconsider. Actually, I wonder if the issue is that `page_map` and `pages` can get out of sync. Let me look at the `resize` function again:

```c
inline bool resize (unsigned int count)
{
    if (unlikely (!successful)) return false;
    if (!pages.resize (count) || !page_map.resize (count))
    {
      pages.resize (page_map.len);
      successful = false;
      return false;
    }
    return true;
}
```

If `pages.resize(count)` succeeds but `page_map.resize(count)` fails, then `pages.resize(page_map.len)` is called. But `page_map.len` is still the old value. So `pages` is shrunk back to `page_map.len`. But `page_map` resize failed, so `page_map.allocated = 0`. Then `successful = false`. So subsequent operations return early.

Hmm, this seems OK.

Let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is in a completely different place. Let me look at the `hb_set_t::next` function again. It calls `page_map.bfind(map, &i)`. If `page_map.len > 0`, then `bfind` reads `arrayZ[mid]`. If `arrayZ` points to the static array (size 1) but `len > 1`, then `arrayZ[mid]` for `mid >= 1` reads beyond the static array.

Wait, but `resize` would have moved `arrayZ` to a heap allocation when `len > 1`. Let me check `alloc`:

```c
inline bool alloc (unsigned int size)
{
    if (unlikely (!allocated))
      return false;

    if (likely (size <= allocated))
      return true;

    /* Reallocate */
    unsigned int new_allocated = allocated;
    while (size >= new_allocated)
      new_allocated += (new_allocated >> 1) + 8;

    Type *new_array = nullptr;

    if (arrayZ == static_array)
    {
      new_array = (Type *) calloc (new_allocated, sizeof (Type));
      if (new_array)
        memcpy (new_array, arrayZ, len * sizeof (Type));
    }
    ...
}
```

So when `size > allocated`, it allocates a new array and copies `len` entries. So `arrayZ` points to the heap array. The new entries are zeroed (calloc). So no uninitialized entries.

